2. The Linux kernel and the vendored kernel patch
-------------------------------------------------

The build workflow fetches the Linux kernel source from the Ubuntu
archive and produces a modified kernel binary. The Linux kernel is NOT
licensed under MIT — it is published primarily under the
GNU General Public License v2.0 (GPL-2.0-only), with individual files
under compatible licenses (notably some user-space-facing headers
under "GPL-2.0 WITH Linux-syscall-note" and a handful of files under
dual licenses). See COPYING and the SPDX tags in the kernel source
tree for the authoritative per-file licensing.

The vendored patch under packaging/kernel/patches/ is a derivative
work of the Linux kernel and is therefore licensed under the same
terms as the files it modifies (GPL-2.0-only), as indicated by its
own SPDX header (`// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only`). The MIT
license above does NOT cover that patch and does NOT cover the
resulting `linux-image-hyperv` binary package.

If you redistribute the built `linux-image-hyperv` .deb you must
comply with the GPL — in particular, you must make the corresponding
source code available to your downstream recipients.


3. The Microsoft Hypervisor and firmware binaries
-------------------------------------------------

The `hyperv` package is built by repackaging closed-source binaries
that Microsoft publishes at:

    https://packages.microsoft.com/azurelinux/3.0/prod/non-oss/

These binaries (hypervisor loader, supporting payload, firmware blobs)
are Microsoft proprietary software. They are NOT covered by the MIT
license above and NOT covered by the GPL. Use of those binaries is
governed by Microsoft's own license terms, which travel with the
upstream Azure Linux package metadata (look for "License:" in the
upstream RPM headers, and follow any links to a EULA referenced
there).

By installing the `hyperv` package you are accepting Microsoft's
license terms for those binaries. This repository merely repackages
the bits into a Debian-installable form; it does not grant you any
additional rights to the binaries, and it does not waive any of
Microsoft's restrictions.

If you cannot or do not wish to accept Microsoft's terms, do not
install the `hyperv` package. The `linux-image-hyperv` kernel package
is independently useful and can be installed on its own.


4. Build-time third-party dependencies
--------------------------------------

The workflows pull in standard Ubuntu packages (build-essential, gcc,
make, etc.), GitHub Actions (actions/checkout, actions/cache,
actions/upload-artifact, actions/github-script), and the Microsoft
RPM payload listed above. Each of those carries its own license; no
part of this repository attempts to relicense them.
